WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.322
ASP.

2
00:00:01.322 --> 00:00:08.792
NET Core Data Protection is a framework service for protecting application data and framework-generated payloads.

3
00:00:08.792 --> 00:00:18.180
It is commonly involved in cookies, antiforgery tokens, password reset tokens, email confirmation tokens, and other protected workflow values.

4
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:24.460
Many applications depend on it even when developers are not directly calling cryptography APIs.

5
00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:30.542
Purpose isolation helps separate protected payloads used for different application purposes.

6
00:00:30.542 --> 00:00:35.566
A value protected for one purpose should not be casually reused for another.

7
00:00:35.566 --> 00:00:42.640
That isolation is part of how Data Protection keeps framework and application payloads separated by intent.

8
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.804
Data Protection depends on key-ring persistence and protection.

9
00:00:46.804 --> 00:00:52.027
In a single developer workstation, defaults may appear to work without thought.

10
00:00:52.027 --> 00:01:03.993
In production, containers, deployment slots, multiple servers, restarts, or cloud hosting can change where keys live and whether protected payloads remain readable across instances.

11
00:01:03.993 --> 00:01:11.595
Multi-instance deployments need shared and durable key rings when protected payloads must be read across instances.

12
00:01:11.595 --> 00:01:21.379
If one instance creates a protected cookie or token and another instance must validate it, both need compatible application identity and key access.

13
00:01:21.379 --> 00:01:25.015
Losing or isolating keys can break users and workflows.

14
00:01:25.015 --> 00:01:29.642
Development defaults may not be suitable for production or containers.

15
00:01:29.642 --> 00:01:39.757
Teams should review key storage, encryption at rest, access control, application name, deployment topology, backup, and rotation behavior before release.

16
00:01:39.757 --> 00:01:47.359
Data Protection is not a general replacement for all encryption, signing, token, database, or key management needs.

17
00:01:47.359 --> 00:01:52.516
It is a maintained framework service for specific protected-payload scenarios.

18
00:01:52.516 --> 00:01:58.664
Use it where it fits, and choose other reviewed mechanisms when the requirement is different.
